The simulation parameters used are listed in \cref{tab: parameter settings}. The evolution of the node weights over the outer iteration step $t$ is illustrated in \cref{fig: Node weight optimization}. The convergence behavior of the largest and Fiedler eigenvalues, as well as the resulting finite condition number, are depicted in \cref{fig: Node weight Eigenvalues}. The algorithm reaches the following optimized node-weighted Laplacian matrix and its corresponding finite condition number:
\begin{gather}
    \label{eq: node weight optimization result}
    \mathcal{L}_{\mathbf{w}^*}=\begin{bsmallmatrix}
        1.8539&         0&         0&   -0.9269  &       0 &  -0.9269 &        0\\
        0&    1.1287&         0&   -0.2822  & -0.2822 &  -0.2822 &  -0.2822\\
        0&         0&    1.2581&   -0.4194  & -0.4194 &  -0.4194 &        0\\
        -0.2442&   -0.2442&   -0.2442&    1.2208  &       0 &  -0.2442 &  -0.2442\\
        0&   -0.9346&   -0.9346&         0  &  1.8692 &        0 &        0\\
        -0.2442&   -0.2442&   -0.2442&   -0.2442  &       0 &   1.2208 &  -0.2442\\
        0&   -0.6192&         0&   -0.6192  &       0 &  -0.6192 &   1.8576\\
    \end{bsmallmatrix},\frac{\lambda_N}{\lambda_2}\left(
    \mathcal{L}_{\mathbf{w}^*}
    \right)=2.5584.
\end{gather}
Compared to the unweighted Laplacian matrix \cref{eq: baseline finite condition number}, the optimization of edge weights has reduced the finite condition number by $40\%$. On the other hand, if one uses an SDP solver to solve the centralized optimization problem \cref{eq: LMI formulation for node weight optimization}, the optimal finite condition number is ${\kappa}=2.5445$, which corresponds to a relative error of $0.55\%$.
