\begin{abstract}
\noindent
Most natural thermodynamic systems operate far from equilibrium, developing persistent currents and organizing into non-equilibrium stationary states (NESSs). However, the principles determining how such systems self-organize by breaking equilibrium symmetries to cope with external and internal constraints remain unclear. Here, we establish a general connection between symmetry breaking and dissipation in mesoscopic stochastic systems described by Langevin dynamics. By introducing a geometric framework based on the inverse diffusion matrix, we decompose the velocity field into excess (gradient) and housekeeping (residual) components. This decomposition provides a natural splitting of entropy production, where the excess part measures how the system reorganizes its internal state-space under non-equilibrium conditions, while the housekeeping part quantifies the degree of detailed-balance violation due to external constraints. We derive an exact equality connecting these two parts, and an associated inequality identifying the accessible thermodynamics. We then perform a weak-noise expansion of the stationary solution to systematically characterize the general geometry of the NESS velocity field, enabling a unified classification of non-equilibrium steady states. We apply this framework to a range of systems, from molecular machines to coupled oscillators, showing how symmetry breaking in trajectory space constrains NESS organization. We also extend our approach to systems with multiplicative fluctuations, a challenging uncharted scenario, deriving the connection between the breaking of additional equilibrium symmetries and the curved (space-dependent) metric. Finally, we show that both the NESS velocity field and stationary distribution can be derived through variational functionals based on excess dissipation.
%excess dissipation acts as a 
%functional shaping the NESS velocity field
%variational functional shaping the NESS velocity field and can be interpreted through a maximum relative entropy principle constrained by Jarzynski statistics.
This work sheds light on the intimate connection between geometric features, dissipative properties, and symmetry breaking, uncovering a classification of NESSs that reflects how emergent organization reflects physical non-equilibrium conditions. Ultimately, our results suggest that excess entropy maximization can serve as a fundamental organizing principle beyond equilibrium thermodynamics.
%where the NESS is the minimum dissipative state compatible with breaking of detailed balance.

%Non-equilibrium thermodynamic systems, driven by external constraints or internal interactions, develop sustained currents and organize into non-equilibrium stationary states (NESSs). Yet, how these systems self-organize to select such steady states, and which principles govern their thermodynamic properties, remains unclear. Here, we establish a general connection between symmetry breaking and dissipation in mesoscopic stochastic systems described by Langevin dynamics. By using the inverse diffusion matrix as a natural metric, we reformulate stochastic thermodynamics in terms of current velocity and generalized free energy (availability), allowing a geometric decomposition of the velocity into housekeeping and excess components. This decomposition provides a natural splitting of entropy production, where the excess part measures how the system reorganizes under non-equilibrium conditions, while the housekeeping part quantifies the degree of detailed-balance violation. We show that excess dissipation can be interpreted as a variational functional shaping the NESS velocity field, linking it to a mimum relative entropy principle constrained by the Jarzynski statistics of housekeeping dissipation. This unifies different variational principles, including the Glansdorff–Prigogine criterion and Onsager’s minimum dissipation principle, and clarifies their physical meaning. By using a weak noise expansion of the adiabatic availability we determine the general geometry of the velocity field, that can be naturally use as a classification system for NESS. We illustrate these ideas in systems ranging from molecular machines to coupled oscillators, revealing how symmetry breaking in trajectory space governs the emergence of NESSs. Overall, our results suggest that minimization of excess or non-adiabatic entropy production may serve as a fundamental organizing principle beyond the classical second law for non-equilibrium systems.
\end{abstract}

