\section{Discussion}

While our results are promising, designing LLM agents for complex systems like the Linux kernel remains challenging.

\textit{Semantic Correctness vs. Crash Resolution.} Crash reproduction serves as a validation signal, but absence of a crash does not guarantee semantic correctness.
Kernel patches must respect subtle invariants (e.g., locking, memory, interrupt safety) not captured in crash reports or as tests. Violating these can lead to latent bugs. Incorporating static checkers or symbolic reasoning post-patch is an open direction.

\textit{Incomplete Observability.} Crash reports from tools like \syzkaller often lack full context—missing inputs, hardware states, and thread interleavings—forcing the agent to reason under partial observability. This hampers root-cause analysis, especially for concurrency bugs. Enhancing agent context with lightweight modeling or hardware-aware tracing may improve accuracy.

\textit{Reproducible Behavior.}  
Agent behavior can be unstable due to limited feedback. We try to minimize this via structured reasoning and forest-based edits, but finer-grained feedback is needed for reliable control.

\textit{Filtering Bad Patches.}  
Automatically discarding (observably) incorrect patches is a promising area. Neural, static, or runtime techniques to detect faulty edits are critical for safe system-level patching, as is incorporation of powerful patch-validation techniques such as targeted fuzzing~\cite{syzdirect,snowplow}.

\textit{Trust and Adoption.}  
Kernel maintainers demand high-quality, explainable patches. Building trust requires LLMs to support reasoned, explainable, and interactive interactions to gain developer acceptance.




% being able to enforce control in the agentic process is a problem, as making the right decisions in real time in the absence of fine-grained feedback is challenging.

\section{Conclusions}

This paper introduced \agent, the first LLM-based software-repair agent targeting the Linux kernel.
The paper presents an evaluation of various repair strategies for kernel bugs and showcases the value
of performing an explicit root-cause analysis before attempting to manipulate the complex logic of the Linux kernel.
We also evaluated \agent's capabilities on still-open bugs, and we found at least two patch suggestions that were considered plausible to resolve the reported bug.
Rather than closing the book on automated Linux bug repair, our work on \agent and \sysplus demonstrates that the overworked and 
overstressed kernel developers may have a new tool, and renewed hope, towards improving kernel reliability and security.



