In this Letter, we study non-Hermitian and quasiperiodic coupled systems. We demonstrate that the eigenvalues corresponding to localized states can indeed emerge within the region of point-gap-loops. Moreover, these states exhibit a very unique boundary-dependent phenomenon, namely AS dualism [see Fig.~\ref{F1AS}]. We have summarized the corresponding characteristics of AS dualism states in Tab.~\ref{T1ASC}.




%Although an old but far-reaching topic, Anderson localization has become an important branch of condensed matter physics~\cite{PWAnderson1958,EAbrahams1979,PALee1985,FEvers2008,ALagendijk2009}. Unlike the edge modes of the non-Hermitian skin effect, the quantum interference due to disorder makes the eigenstates localized in bulk. The competition between Anderson localization and non-Hermitian has attracted attention in recent studies~\cite{YLiu2021,HJiang2019,ZGong2018,XCai2021,XCai2022,LZTang2021,LZTang2022,SLJiang2023,ZQZhang2023,HLiu2023,SLonghi2019a,XPJiang2024,LWang2024a,LWang2024b,LJZhai2020,APAcharya2024,TLiu2020,XTong2025,SZLi2024}. In non-reciprocal disordered models, the onset of the localization transition is accompanied by the closure of the point gap, and the spectral winding number undergoes a transition from non-zero to zero~\cite{HJiang2019}. Therefore, the general knowledge is that Anderson localized states do not depend on boundary conditions and that the spectral winding number is always zero through the eigenvalue of the localized state~\cite{HJiang2019,SZLi2024,YLiu2021}. However, it is still unknown whether localized states can exist within the point gap and whether skin effects are still followed in the point gap.


%In this letter, we reveal an AS duality with a point gap topology and disorder acting together. As shown in Tab.~\ref{tab:wave-localization}, the AS dual state exhibits Anderson localization with localization in the bulk for PBCs, and skin effects with localization on the boundary for OBCs. This AS duality state can be realized by coupling Hatano-Nelson (HN)~\cite{NHatano1996} and Aubry-Andr\'e (AA)~\cite{SAubry1980} chains. By the transfer matrix, we can analytically obtain the LE of the localized mode. We find that the AS dual state and the skin state have the same, one-sided LE. We also explore the behavior of AS duality in dynamics. We find that under PBCs, the wave packet possesses both localized and unidirectional transmission properties. Under OBCs, the wave packet evolves towards the boundary and exhibits skin effects, and it also exhibits localized striping over long periods of time. Finally, we explore the occupancy-dependent AS duality in the context of many-body interactions.

