\section{VI. Topological circuit realization in 1D without interactions}
By forming circuits with components such as capacitors, resistors, and inductors, the circuit system can realize an arbitrary non-interacting tight-binding system~\cite{CHLee2018}. The basic properties of circuit systems are described by Ohm’s law and Kirchhoff’s law. Then, we have $\mathbf{I}=\mathcal{J} \mathbf{V} $, where $\mathbf{I}=(I_{a,1}~I_{a,2}~\dots~I_{a,N},~I_{b,1}~I_{b,2}~\dots~I_{b,N})^{T}$ and $\mathbf{V}=(V_{a,1}~V_{a,2}~\dots~V_{a,N},~V_{b,1}~V_{b,2}~\dots~V_{b,N})^{T}$, where $\mathcal{J}$ is the Laplacian of the circuit.

%%%%%%
%%%%%%
 \begin{figure*}[htbp]
	\centering	\includegraphics[width=15cm]{fig_exp.pdf}
	\caption{(a) Schematic circuit simulation of Hamiltonian~\eqref{1DHNAA}, where blue and yellow circles simulate lattice sites on the HN and AA chains, respectively. (b) VFs are used as active devices with mismatched input and output currents to realize non-reciprocal transmission in HN chains.}\label{F4}
\end{figure*}
