\paragraph{OOD evaluation.}
As shown in Table~\ref{tab:ood_results}, the fine-tuned MAE (MAE-FT) had the highest performance over all other methods across traits, achieving a slight improvement in $R^2$ relative to the supervised baseline ($0.31$ vs. $0.24$), along with the lowest average nRMSE. Other competing methods, such as SR-GAN and RTM-AE, provided modest gains over the supervised baseline. Interestingly, MAE with linear probing (MAE-LP) underperformed in the OOD setting, highlighting that fine-tuning is essential to adapt pretrained features to novel spectral distributions.
The corresponding scatter plot of the observed and predicted trait values from the different methods is presented in Appendix~\ref{fig:scatter}.

\section{Conclusions and perspectives}
\label{sec:conclusions}
% \com{TODO: remind global contribs and conclusion: leveraging GreeSpec as a unique pretraining dataset, our pretrained MAE shown consistently best performances over all benchmark outperforming the state-of-the-art supervised method...}

% \com{TODO: add limitation about label datasets: geographic biais, but we hopt that our pretrained models will be helpful when data in new locations will be labeled.}
In this study, we introduce \textbf{GreenHyperSpectra}, a large-scale cross-sensor and cross-ecosystem spectral dataset designed to train machine learning models for plant trait prediction from hyperspectral data. Leveraging GreenHyperSpectra as a pretraining resource, we demonstrated that models using MAE consistently outperformed all other benchmarked methods, including the fully supervised baseline, across a variety of settings. The adaptability of MAE models enables their application to multi-scale remote sensing platforms, including drone, airborne, and satellite imagery, paving the way to investigate how learned spectral features, derived from a heterogeneous spectral dataset, generalize across varying spatial resolutions. MAEs also serve as a strong foundation for advanced transfer learning architectures aimed at improving predictive performance.

%While our work demonstrates the potential of spectral representation learning, adapting pretrained encoders to sensor configurations outside the full- and half-range settings explored in this study remains open for further investigation.
%The inductive biases of the MAE architecture demonstrate particular promise for cross-domain adaptation across heterogeneous sensing modalities through targeted fine-tuning strategies.
%Our work also contributes towards building a global pretraining dataset to motivate the development of spectral embeddings while highlighting critical dataset biases affecting cross-domain generalization. 
%Despite performance improvements, observed run-to-run variance suggests challenges in learning stable representations from heterogeneous ecological data distributions. Future research should prioritize expanding multi-domain spectral datasets with improved coverage across biomes and sensing conditions to enhance representation transferability and mitigate distribution shifts. 
%Furthermore, the annotated datasets used for the evaluation still exhibit geographical and ecosystem-level biases. Expanding the availability and coverage of annotated samples will be essential to improve model generalization. 
% While trait prediction from Earth observation generally has no direct negative societal impact, it is important to acknowledge that such insights can be applied in ways that prioritize agricultural overproduction, for instance, through trait-driven genetic modifications, at the expense of ecological balance.
%Nevertheless, we expect that our pretrained models from GreenHyperSpectra will remain valuable as more labeled data become available from underrepresented regions.
%Overall, this study shows that semi- and self-supervised methods with large-scale pretraining datasets are key to further advance the monitoring of ecosystems and their plant functional traits.

While we explore default sensor configurations (VNIR+SWIR and VNIR), extending pretrained encoders to other spectral ranges remains open for future work.
The MAE architecture shows promise for cross-domain adaptation across heterogeneous sensing modalities through fine-tuning strategies. 
We contribute towards global pretraining datasets for spectral embeddings while highlighting critical biases affecting generalization. 
Despite improvements, run-to-run variance reveals challenges in learning stable representations from various ecological data distributions. 
Future research should expand multi-domain spectral datasets across biomes and sensing conditions to enhance transferability and address geographical and ecosystem-level biases in annotated data. 
Nevertheless, our pretrained models from GreenHyperSpectra will remain valuable as labeled data from underrepresented regions increases. This study confirms that semi- and self-supervised methods with large-scale pretraining are essential for advancing ecosystem monitoring.

