\section{Experimental Details}\label{A}
\subsection{Zero-shot estimation models.}
In this subsection, we provide details on the zero-shot estimation model used in our pseudo-labeling framework. 
Notably, any other zero-shot estimation model with the same functionality can be used, as our proposed method does not depend on a specific model.

\paragrapht{Open-vocabulary 2D detector.} 
In this paper, we use Grounded SAM~\cite{ren2024grounded} for open-vocabulary 2D detection and segmentation, which combines Grounding DINO~\cite{liu2025grounding} and SAM~\cite{ravi2024sam}. 
For all experiments, we use publicly available weights and prompts with a predefined set of class labels. 
In Tab.~\ref{tab:supp_promt}, we provide a list of prompts used to generate pseudo-labels for each dataset.
In all cases, we set the text threshold to $0.25$ and the box threshold to $0.3$. 
The model generally demonstrates strong open-world performance; however, as seen in Fig. 2 of the main paper and Fig.~\ref{fig:supp_gsam_tracking}, it produces inconsistent predictions depending on factors such as noise, occlusion, and distance. This highlights the necessity of our proposed label improvement method.

