\subsection{Error metrics.}
\paragrapht{Matching.}
We quantify pseudo-label errors using a formulation inspired by the nuScenes~\cite{nuscenes} evaluation metrics.
In 3D pseudo-label generation, object detection performance primarily depends on the foundational 2D detector, such as Grounded SAM~\cite{ren2024grounded}.
To identify true positives, we match pseudo-labels to ground truth using 2D IoU (intersection over union), rather than the 2D center distance on the ground plane used in nuScenes~\cite{nuscenes} or the 3D IoU matching employed in KITTI~\cite{kitti} and Omni3D~\cite{brazil2023omni3d}.
Accordingly, we report detection performance using 2D average precision ($\mathrm{AP_{2D}}$), computed over 10 IoU thresholds $\{0.5, 0.55, ..., 0.95\}$, following the COCO evaluation~\cite{lin2014microsoft} protocol.

\paragrapht{TP metrics.}
We identify true positives using a 2D IoU threshold of $0.5$ and compute errors for each 3D box attribute per true positive instance. This approach separates detection performance from 3D attribute inaccuracies, particularly depth estimation errors, enabling a focused analysis of factors affecting 3D pseudo-label quality.
To evaluate localization accuracy, we calculate the Euclidean distance in BEV (in meters) and report the average translation error (ATE).
For object scale accuracy, we use the average scale error (ASE), defined as $1-\mathrm{IoU_{3D}}$, computed in the object's local coordinate system to ensure it is unaffected by localization errors.
Lastly, we measure orientation accuracy using the average orientation error (AOE), defined as the absolute yaw angle difference (in radians).



\subsection{Baselines} % you can change the name 
In this subsection, we provide details on running the baseline methods.

\paragrapht{Pseudo-labeling method.}
OVM3D-Det~\cite{huangtraining} was the first to introduce a training-free monocular 3D object detection approach by leveraging foundational knowledge from an open-vocabulary 2D detector~\cite{ren2024grounded} and a monocular depth estimator~\cite{unidepth}.
Their method estimates object center locations directly from a single pseudo-LiDAR observation and determines orientation using PCA.
For dimension estimation, they search for the optimal bounding box size by tracing a ray from the camera through multiple proposal boxes, incorporating fixed prior size information.
For comparison, we use the original implementation and pseudo-labels provided by the authors without any modifications.

\paragrapht{Multi-domain M3OD method.}
Cube R-CNN~\cite{brazil2023omni3d} is the first supervised method to address domain generalization in monocular 3D object detection.
It introduces Omni3D, a large-scale benchmark that spans both indoor and outdoor datasets.
The model builds upon Faster R-CNN~\cite{ren2015faster}, incorporating a cube head to regress 3D box attributes.
For qualitative comparisons, we use the official pre-trained weights provided by the authors.

\paragrapht{Single-domain M3OD methods.}
Single-domain methods assume access to 3D ground truth boxes during training, tailored for a specific scene and a fixed camera setup.
We evaluate two supervised approaches: GUPNet~\cite{gupnet} and MonoDETR~\cite{zhang2023monodetr}, both of which incorporate uncertainty modeling for depth prediction.
Geometric uncertainty estimation is particularly beneficial when training with unreliable pseudo-label depths, as it enhances learning stability.
For a fair comparison, we use the original training settings without modifying the models.




