\subsection{Failure Cases}
Our proposed pseudo-labeling method can face performance limitations due to three key factors: 1) minimal variation in observation angle, 2) challenging mask association caused by persistent object clutter, and 3) unreliable object detection due to excessive noise.
Our 2D object tracking across frames generally leads to good 3D shape reconstruction~(the first and the second row of Fig.~\ref{fig:supp_pseudo-lidar}). However, as seen in the last row of Fig.~\ref{fig:supp_pseudo-lidar}, when the object's observation angle remains nearly constant across frames, shape enhancement and the resulting performance gains can be minimal. Secondly, if objects remain persistently cluttered, mask association based on intersection over union (IoU) may fail, resulting in ill-registered pseudo-LiDAR. The top figure of Fig.~\ref{fig:supp_failure} demonstrates a failure case where both mask association and pseudo-LiDAR completion are challenging due to the aforementioned reasons. Lastly, since our pseudo-labeling method relies on the initial detections from the 2D detector~\cite{ren2024grounded}, labeling becomes difficult if the 2D detector consistently fails to detect the object across frames. The bottom figure of Fig.~\ref{fig:supp_failure} illustrates a case where the 2D detector fails to detect an object due to severe noise caused by light saturation. Addressing these issues will be crucial for ensuring the robust application of our proposed method. However, unlike the KITTI evaluation experiments where the number of input frames is limited~($3$ preceding frames), these limitations are likely to be mitigated as frame variations increase.

\subsection{In-the-Wild Testing}
Since 3D pseudo-label generation depends on 2D foundational models that have been trained on extensive datasets, we carefully select video datasets for a fair evaluation, ensuring they are not part of these foundational models.
In Fig.~\ref{fig:supp_results1}, we test on diverse scenes with various camera angles from Pexels~\cite{pexels2025}.
Additionally, in Fig.~\ref{fig:supp_results2}, we use the recent MOSE~\cite{ding2023mose} dataset to evaluate on unusual classes that are not labeled in most 3D object detection datasets.
A large-scale multi-domain method, Cube R-CNN~\cite{brazil2023omni3d}, struggles when the camera viewpoint diverges from its training data and cannot detect unseen object classes.
The previous pseudo-labeling method, OVM3D-Det~\cite{huangtraining}, faces challenges due to fixed prior sizes and neglecting depth uncertainty in difficult scenarios, which results in abnormal bounding boxes when visualized on the image plane.

