\begin{abstract}
We analyse the problem of meeting times for interdependent stochastic agents: random walkers whose behaviour is stochastic but controlled by their selections from some set of allowed actions, and the inference problem of when these agents will be in the same state for the first time. 
We consider the case where we are epistemically uncertain about the selected actions of these agents, and show how their behaviour can be modelled using imprecise Markov chains. This allows us to use results and algorithms from the literature, to exactly compute bounds on their meeting time, which are tight with respect to our epistemic uncertainty models. We focus on the two-agent case, but discuss how it can be naturally extended to an arbitrary number of agents, and how the corresponding combinatorial explosion can be partly mitigated by exploiting symmetries inherent in the problem.
\begin{comment}
We start by discussing the meeting-time problem for agents modelled as two independent Markov chains---finding the first time at which both chains occupy the same state---and recall how this problem is equivalent to a hitting-time problem on the Cartesian product of their state spaces. 
Then, we consider the problem of two agents selecting their transition probabilities depending on the others' state and who are in this way interdependent. 
By constructing a joint imprecise Markov chain on the product space to model this problem, we directly harness the existing theory of hitting times for imprecise Markov chains to obtain an explicit expression for upper expected meeting times as well as an iterative algorithm for computing them. 
We then extend this framework to an arbitrary number of interdependent agents, showing that the same hitting-time machinery scales naturally to characterise and compute their meeting times.
\end{comment}
