We will denote by $x\uppereach y$ if $y$ is upper reachable from $x$.
Analogously, $y\in \ZZ$ is \textit{lower reachable} from $x\in \ZZ$ 
if there exists $n\in \N$ such that $[\underlineT^n\one_{\{y\}}](x)>0$.
%i.e. for all $T\in\T$ there exists $n\in \N$ such that $[T^n \one_{\{y\}}](x)>0$. 
We will denote by $x\lowereach y$ if $y$ is lower reachable from $x$. 


Given a set of transition matrices $\T$ and a target set $A\subset \ZZ$, we say that the reachability condition (R1) holds if
\[
\text{(R1)}:\quad \forall\,x\in A^c: x\lowereach A
\]
Under (R1), the upper expected hitting time \(\overline{h}^\T_A(x)\) is finite for all \(x\in A^c\)~\cite{krak2021comphit}.
%
If (R1) does not hold, i.e. there exists a set of states $\A_\T\subset A^c$ such that $\forall x\in \A_\T$, $x \notlowereach A$, then for all $x\in \A_T$ it holds that  $\overline{h}^\T_A(x)=+\infty$. This is due to the fact that for all $x\in \A_\T$ there exists $T\in \T$ such that for all $n\in \N$ it holds that $[T^n\one_A](x)=0$. The set $\A_\T$ is often called the set of (lower) \textit{absorbing states} of $\T$.

Let us denote with $\U_\T$ the set of all states of $A^c\setminus \A_\T$ that upper reach a state in $\A_\T$, i.e.
\begin{equation*}
    \U_\T \coloneqq\{x\in A^c\setminus \A_T \mid  x\uppereach \A_\T\}.
\end{equation*}
The following theorem characterizes upper 
%expected 
%saving space removing "expected"
hitting times for all states of $\ZZ$.
\begin{theorem}\label{th:rickcut}
    Let $\T$ be a set of transition matrices and let $\A_T$ and $\U_\T$ be as defined above. Then, for all $x\in \B_\T:=\A_\T \cup \U_\T$ it holds that $\overline{h}^\T_A(x)=+\infty$. Moreover, there exists a matrix $\tilde{T}\in \T$ such that 
    \[
    \overline{h}^\T_A|_{A^c\setminus \B_\T}=(I-\tilde{T}|_{A^c\setminus \B_\T})^{-1}{1},
    \]
    where $I$ denotes the identity matrix and 1 the vector of all ones. 
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
    It follows from Krak et al.~\cite[Theorem 12]{krak2019hitting} that there always exists a matrix $\tilde{T}\in \T$ such that $\overline{h}^\T_A=h^{\tilde{T}}$, where $h^{\tilde{T}}$ is the minimal non-negative solution of the linear system $h^{\tilde{T}}=\one_{A^c}+\one_{A^c}\cdot \tilde{T} h^{\tilde{T}}$. If $x\in \U_\T$ the Markov chain $\PP_{\tilde{T}}$ that starts in $x$ has positive probability of hitting the absorbing set $\A_\T$ thus positive probability of never reaching $A$. This implies that the upper expected hitting time for all $x\in \U_\T$ is infinite. 

    Observe that for all $x\in A^c\setminus \B_\T$ and for all $T\in \T$ holds that $ \forall n\in \N: [T^n \one_{\U_\T\cup \A_\T}](x)=0$. Therefore, we can restrict $\tilde{T}$ on $\tilde{\ZZ}:=\ZZ\setminus \B_\T$ as the Markov chain $\PP_{\tilde{T}}$ starting from any state in $\tilde{\ZZ}$ never leaves $\tilde{\ZZ}$. Using Proposition 6 of Krak~\cite{krak2021comphit} we are able to conclude that $h^{\tilde{T}}|_{\tilde{\ZZ}\setminus A}=(I-\tilde{T}|_{\tilde{\ZZ}\setminus A})^{-1}{\textbf{1}}$ which is what we wanted.\qed
\end{proof}
An analogous result holds for lower expected hitting times: in this case, the definitions of $\A_\T$ and $\U_\T$ are obtained by swapping upper and lower reachability in their respective constructions.
