\subsubsection{A Computational Method.}
Upper- and lower expected hitting times can be computed efficiently using iterative algorithms~\cite{krak2021comphit, krak2019hitting}. One such algorithm is the following: starting from any extreme point $T_1$ of $\T$, let $h_n$ be the unique solution of the linear system $h_n=\one_{A^c}+\one_{A^c}\cdot T_n h_n$ and let $T_{n+1}$ be an extreme point of $\T$ such that $\overline{T}h_{n}=T_{n+1}h_n$. Krak~\cite{krak2021comphit} showed that, under (R1), the sequence $(h_n)_{n\in\N}$ converges to $\overline{h}^{\T}_A$. 

If there is a non-empty set $\A_\T=\{x\in A^c \mid x\notlowereach A \}$ then, as Theorem~\ref{th:rickcut} shows, we may restrict our analysis on $A^c\setminus \B_\T$ as elsewhere the upper expected hitting time is either zero or infinite. Let $\SSS$ be a subset of $\T$ defined as 
\begin{equation*}
    \SSS\coloneqq\{T\in\T \mid \forall x\in A^c\setminus \B_\T: [T\one_{\B_\T}](x)=0 \}.
\end{equation*}
For each $S\in \SSS$ consider the restriction of $S$ on $\B_\T^c$ 
\begin{equation*}
    S|_{\B_\T^c} \;=\;\bigl(S{(i, j)}\bigr)_{\,i,j\in \B_\T^c}\;\in\R^{|\B_\T^c|\times|\B_\T^c|},
\end{equation*}
the submatrix of $S$ indexed by $\B_\T^c$. Then, the set of matrices $\SSS|_{\B_\T^c}$ containing the restriction on $\B_\T^c$ of all matrices in $\SSS$ satisfies (R1). Therefore, we may apply Krak's algorithm to compute the upper expected hitting time for all non-trivial states of $\ZZ$.

Identifying the sets $\A_\T$ and $ \U_\T$ can be done using an adaption of De Bock's algorithms~\cite{debock2017imprecise}. In particular, $\A_\T$ can be found adapting Algorithm 2 in~\cite{debock2017imprecise} meanwhile $\U_\T$ can be found adapting Algorithm 1  in~\cite{debock2017imprecise} with $\A_\T$ as target set. 

