\subsubsection{Communicating and Cyclic Classes}
In a Markov chain with state space \(\ZZ\) and transition matrix \(T\), we say that two states \(x,y\in\ZZ\) \emph{communicate}, written \(x\leftrightarrow^T y\), if each is accessible from the other via some sequence of positive‐probability transitions, i.e.\ there exist \(m,n\in \N\) with \((T^m)_{xy}>0\) and \((T^n)_{yx}>0\).  This relation is an equivalence relation on \(\ZZ\), whose equivalence classes are called \emph{communicating classes}.  
A communicating class \(C\subseteq\ZZ\) is said to be \emph{closed} if no transitions leave it i.e. 
\(
 T_{xy} \;=\; 0 \;
\text{ for every }x\in C \text{ and } y\notin C.
\)
Otherwise we call \(C\) \emph{transient}, since every process in it will eventually “leak” out of \(C\) into other states or classes.  

Within each communicating class \(C\), we define the \emph{period} of a state \(x\in C\) as
\[
d^T(x) \; :=\; \gcd\bigl\{\,n\ge1 : (T^n)_{xx} > 0\bigr\}.
\]
This period is constant across all states in the same communicating class, so we refer unambiguously to “the” period \(d^T\) of the class. The communicating class \(C\) further decomposes into \(d^T\) disjoint \emph{cyclic subclasses} $R_0,R_1,\dots,R_{d^T-1}$ such that transitions from any state in \(R_k\) lead only to states in \(R_{k+1 \bmod d^T}\), yielding a deterministic cyclic itinerary through the \(d^T\) subsets; when \(d^T=1\), the class is called \emph{aperiodic}.  

Thus for a finite Markov chain on \(\ZZ\) with transition matrix \(T\), the state‐space breaks into communicating classes, each of which is either closed or transient, and each communicating class further refines into cyclic subclasses according to the period of its states.

