\subsubsection{Upper Classes for Imprecise Markov Chains.}
It is well known that a (precise) Markov chain partitions its state space into communicating classes, each of which can be further decomposed into cyclic subclasses~\cite{norrisbook1997markov, kemeny1976finite}. We employ the same decomposition for imprecise Markov chains, following the approach in~\cite{tjoens2021average}. 

Given a set of transition matrices $\T$, we say that two states $x,y \in \ZZ$ \textit{upper communicate}, written $x \upcomm y$, if $x\uppereach y$ and $y\uppereach x$ i.e. there exist $n,m \in \N$ with $[\overline{T}^n \one_{y}](x)>0$ and $[\overline{T}^m \one_{x}](y)>0$. 
%This means that $x$ and $y$ upper communicate if (and only if) there exists $T\in \T$ such $x\leftrightarrow^T y$. 

As upper communicability is an equivalence relation, we can naturally partition the state space $\ZZ$ into upper communicating classes (UCCs). 
We distinguish three types of upper communicating classes: closed, closable and transient. An UCC $C$ is said to be 
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textit{closed} if every transition matrix in $\T$ has zero probability of leaving the class i.e. $[\overlineT \one_{\{y\}}](x)=0$ for all $x\in C$ and $y\notin C$;
    \item \textit{transient} if every transition matrix in $\T$ has positive probability of leaving the class i.e. there are $x\in C$ and $y\notin C$ such that $[\underlineT \one_{y}](x)>0$;
    \item \textit{closable} if, depending on the choice of transition matrix \(T\in \T\), \(C\) can be either closed or transient. This means that $[\underlineT \one_{y}](x)=0$ for all $x\in C, y\notin C$, and there are $x'\in C, y'\notin C$ such that $[\overlineT \one_{\{y\}}](x)>0$.
\end{itemize}

We can define the upper period of a state $x\in \ZZ$ as 
\begin{equation*}
    \od(x) \;=\; \gcd\bigl\{\,n\ge1 : [\overline{T}^n\one_{\{x\}}](x) > 0\bigr\}.
\end{equation*}
As in the precise case, this value is common for all states in the same UCC. We say that an UCC is upper aperiodic if its upper period is one. 
We shall also note that $\od(x)$ is the minimum value among all possible choices of transition matrix $T\in \T$, i.e. $\od(x)=\inf\{d^T(x) \mid T\in \T\}$.
Each UCC $C$ of upper period $\od$ can be further partitioned into $\od$ disjoint upper cyclic subclasses $R_0,\dots, R_{\od-1}$ such that transitions form any state in $R_k$ using any matrix $T\in \T$ lead only in states in $R_{k+1 \bmod \od}$.
