\subsubsection{Characterization of States in the Product Space.}
With these definitions we can finally characterize when two interdependent imprecise stochastic processes meet. We start by noting that if the two processes start in $(x,y)\in \ZZ^2$ and both $x$ and $y$ are in the same upper cyclic subclass of a closed (or closable) UCC then their lower expected meeting time is finite. 
Conversely, if the two processes do not start in the same upper cyclic subclass of a closed (or closable) UCC then their lower expected meeting time is finite if and only if there exists a $n\in \N$ such that $[\overline{\Delta}^n \one_\Rcal](x,y)=1$, where $\Rcal\subseteq \ZZ^2$ contains $D=\{(k,k)\mid k\in \ZZ\}$ and all pairs of states that are in the same upper cyclic subclass of a closed (or closable) UCC. This means that the two processes meet if there is at least one strategy, i.e. families of transition matrices $T^1, \dots, T^ {N}$ and $S^1, \dots, S^{N}$, that guarantees that they will meet (hit $D$) or fall in the same upper cyclic subclass of a closed (or closable) UCC.

Every pair of states for which every possible strategy leaves a positive probability of never meeting has infinite lower expected meeting time. The set containing all such pairs is exactly $\A_{\T^2}\cup \U_{T^2}$. 
