\section{Meeting Times of Multiple Interdependent Agents}\label{sec:manyagents}
Consider $m\in \N, m>2$ interdependent stochastic agents 
%$\PP^1,\dots, \PP^m$ 
on the same state space $\ZZ$ with set of transition matrices $\T$. Each agent looks at the position of all other agents to determine which selection to make. For every agent $j\in [m]$, her selections are identified with a collection of ${N}^{m-1}$ transition matrices, $(T_j^{(z_i)_{i\in [m], i\ne j}})$ where $(z_i)_{i\in [m], i\ne j}$ represents the states of all other agents.
%and $[m]=\{1,\dots, m\}$.
To find the upper (and lower) expected meeting time of these stochastic agents, we consider the joint homogenous imprecise Markov chain $(Z^1,\dots, Z^m)$ on
%$\ZZ^m=\bigtimes_{i=1}^m\ZZ$. 
$\ZZ^m=\ZZ\times\dots\times \ZZ$, $m$ times.
Similarly as before, we define a set of transition matrices $\T^m$ on the space $\ZZ^m$ as follows
\begin{equation*}
    \T^m\coloneqq\Bigl\{\Delta_m\in \R^{{N}^m \times {N}^m} \,\Big\vert\,\Delta_m((z_{1:m}),(z'_{1:m}))=\prod_{j=1}^m T_j^{(z_i)_{i\ne j}}(z_j,z'_j)\Bigr\},
\end{equation*}
where, for each $j=1,\dots, m$, we vary over all possible selections of the agents. 
%Each matrix $\Delta_m\in \T^m$ is therefore defined using $m{N}^{m-1}$ transition matrices on $\ZZ$. 
As before, under the assumption that $\T$ is compact, convex and has SSR, the set $\T^m$ is compact and has separately specified rows. The upper (and lower) expected meeting time of $m$ interdependent stochastic agents with set of transition matrices $\T$ can be seen as the upper (and lower) hitting time of a homogenous imprecise Markov chain on the space $\ZZ^m$ with set of transition matrices $\T^m$ and target set $D^m:=\{(k,\dots,k) \mid k\in N\}$.

As in the case with two agents, by taking the convex hull of \(\T^m\) and defining the corresponding upper (and lower) transition operator, we obtain a characterization of the upper (and lower) meeting time analogous to that in Eq.~\eqref{eq:lowermeetchara}.
Moreover, this also allows us to apply iterative algorithms of Krak~\cite{krak2021comphit} to compute the upper (and lower) expected meeting time, as taking the convex hull leaves the upper (and lower) expected meeting time unchanged.
% Note, however, that the state space \(\ZZ^m\) grows exponentially with \(m\), and if \(\operatorname{ext}(\T)\) denotes the number of extreme points of \(\T\), then \(\T^m\) has on the order of \(\bigl(\operatorname{ext}(\T)\bigr)^{m\,N^{\,m-1}}\)extreme points, potentially rendering computations infeasible for large \(m\). 

\subsection{Symmetry Reduction}
%If $2<k<m$ stochastic processes are in the same state $z\in \ZZ$ their transition matrix that minimizes meeting time is the same for all $k$ processes and depends only on the position of the other $m-k$ processes. Similarly, these other $m-k$ processes will have the same optimal transition matrix whether in $z$ there are 1 or $k$ distinct processes. Therefore, the cost to compute lower expected meeting times for $m>N$ interdependent imprecise stochastic processes is equivalent to that of $N$ such processes, thereby reducing the computational burden for large values of \( m \).
The state space $\ZZ^m$ grows exponentially with $m$.
%With $m>2$ interdependent stochastic processes the state space $\ZZ^m$ becomes exponentially large. 
Exploiting the symmetry drastically reduces  the number of states to consider as 
\begin{equation*}
|\ZZ^m/S_m|= \binom{N+m-1}{m}\ll {N}^m=|\ZZ^m|,
\end{equation*}
where $S_m$ is the symmetric group of degree $m$. In other words, in the space $\ZZ^m/S_m$ all permutations of an $m$-tuple are identified.
%where $\sim$ is the ezuivalence relation that identifies two $m$-tuple if and only if their underlying sets of elements are equal.
As in the case $m=2$, every matrix $\Delta_m:\R^{|\ZZ^m/S_m|}\to \R^{|\ZZ^m/S_m|}$ is now defined using only $N^{m-1}$ matrices from $\T$, as, the $m$ families of transition matrices coincide. 

Moreover, if $2\le k<m$ stochastic agents are in the same state $z\in \ZZ$ their selected action that minimizes or maximizes the meeting time is the same for all $k$ agents and depends only on the position of the other $m-k$ agents. Similarly, these other $m-k$ agents will have the same optimal selection whether in $z$ there are 1 or $k$ distinct agents. Therefore, the cost to compute upper expected meeting times for $m>N$ interdependent stochastic agents is equivalent to that of $N$ such agents, thus reducing the computational burden for large values of \( m \).
