		\section{Conclusion}\label{sec:conclusion} 
		In this work, we introduced the network behavioral-feedback SIR epidemic model (NBF-SIR), a networked SIR model where the interaction matrix depends in feedback on the epidemic state, to incorporate behavioral adaptations such as voluntary social distancing. We characterized the stability of the equilibria, and analyze how the structure of the interaction matrix affects the stability region. We then considered the transient of dynamics for rank-1 interaction matrices, showing that, under certain assumptions on the feedback mechanism, the dynamics admits a unimodal aggregate infection curve.  

		Future research directions include the analysis of the transient dynamics for matrices with higher rank, e.g., to include homophily and heterogeneous interactions, and the design of optimal control strategies for the NBF-SIR model that exploit the properties of the model that have been established in this paper.

