		\section{Proof of Proposition \ref{prop:basic}}\label{app}
		(i) Since $A$ is $\mc C'$, the vector field of \eqref{eq:behavioral-network-SIR-compact} is $\mc C^1$ with respect to $(x,y)$. Hence, existence and uniqueness of the solution is a standard result \cite[Chapter~I.3]{hale}. %, \cite[Chapter~3.2]{knauf} \tcr{scegliamo una sola reference, non serve averne 2}. 
		To prove that $\mc S$ is positively invariant, note that for every point belonging to the boundary of $\mc S$, the vector field is either tangent, or points inside the set $\mc S$. Therefore for $(x(0),y(0))$ in $\mc S$, it follows from Nagumo’s Theorem \cite{blanchini} that $(x(t),y(t))$ belongs to $\mc S$ for all $t > 0$, which means that $\mc S$ is a positively invariant set. \\
		%In particular, this implies that the solution is globally defined. \\

		(ii) Since $\mc S$ is positively invariant, $x(t)$, $y(t)$ and $A(x(t),y(t))$ have non-negative entries for every time $t$. Therefore, 
		$$\dot{x}_i(t) = -x_i(t) \sum_{j=1}^n A_{ij}(x,y)y_j(t) \leq 0$$ for all $i = 1, \dots, n$ and $t \geq 0$. By integrating the first equation of \eqref{eq:behavioral-network-SIR}, for each node $i$ we get
		$$ x_i(t) = x_i(0) \exp \Big(- \int_{0}^{t} \sum_{j=1}^n A_{ij}(x(\tau), y(\tau)) y_j(\tau) \de \tau \Big),$$
		hence $x_i(t)>0$ for every $t\ge0$ if and only if $x_i(0)>0$. 
		%\indent(iv) Assume by contradiction the existence of $i \in \{1, \dots, n\}$ and $T > 0$ such that $y_i(T) = 0$. Then, \eqref{eq:behavioral-network-SIR} implies that $\dot{y}_i(T) \geq 0$, which yields $\dot{y}_i(t) = 0$ for all $t \in [0, T]$ otherwise $y_i(t)$ would be negative for some $t < T$. 
		%By \eqref{eq:behavioral-network-SIR}, this would imply that $y_j(t) = 0$ for all $t \in [0, T]$ for all $j$ such that $A_{ij}(x,y) > 0$. We could iterate this argument and, using the irreducibility of $A(x,y)$, we would reach the contradiction that $y(t) = \0$ for all $t \in [0, T]$. \\

		(iii) Since $\dot y_i(t) \ge - \gamma y_i(t)$, Gronwall's inequality implies $y_i(t) \ge y_i(0) e^{-\gamma t} > 0$.  

		(iv) A point $(x^*, y^*)\in \mc S$ is an equilibrium if and only if 
		\begin{equation*}
			\0 = -   [x^*] A(x^*,y^*) y^*\,,\qquad 
			\0 =   [x^*] A(x^*,y^*) y^* - \gamma y^*\,.
		\end{equation*}
		This implies that the points of the form $(x^*, \0)$, are all equilibria, referred to as a \textit{disease-free equilibria}. Moreover, these are the only equilibria, since every point with $y^* \neq \0$ would not satisfy the equilibrium conditions given above. 

		(v) Observe that the sum of the first two equations of \eqref{eq:behavioral-network-SIR} is $\dot{x}_i(t) + \dot{y}_i(t) = -\gamma y_i(t)\leq0$ for every $i$,
		hence $x_i(t)+y_i(t)$ is a non-increasing function.
		Since $x_i(t)$ and $x_i(t) + y_i(t)$ are both non-increasing functions, they both admit a limit
		$$x_{i}^* := \lim_{t \to +\infty} x_i(t),\;\;\; \xi_{i}^* := \lim_{t \to +\infty} [x_i(t)+y_i(t)]\,.$$
		As a consequence, the following limit 
		\begin{equation*}
			y_{i}^*= \lim_{t \to +\infty} y_i(t)=\xi_{i}^*-x_{i}^*\geq 0
		\end{equation*}
		exists.	Suppose now by contradiction that $y_{i}^* >0$. This means that there exist $T, \epsilon >0$ such that $y_i(t) > \epsilon$, for all $t \geq T$. 
		Then, we get 
		\begin{align*}
			\xi_{i}^*&=\lim_{t \to +\infty} [x_i(t) + y_i(t) ]\\
			%&= x(0) + y(0) - \gamma \int_{0}^{\infty} y_i(\tau) d\tau \\
			&= x_i(0) + y_i(0) - \gamma \lim_{t \to +\infty} \int_{0}^{t} y_i(\tau) d\tau \\
			&< x_i(0) + y_i(0) - \gamma \lim_{t \to +\infty} \int_{T}^{t} y_i(\tau) d\tau \\[6pt]
			&< x_i(0) + y_i(0) - \gamma \epsilon \lim_{t \to +\infty} (t- T) = - \infty
		\end{align*}
		As this is a contradiction, it must be $y_{i}^* =0$ and this is valid for every $i = 1,\dots,n$. 
		Therefore, for every $(x(0), y(0))$ in $\mc S$, the trajectory $(x(t), y(t))$ converges to an equilibrium of the form $(x^*, \0)$. Moreover, from point (ii), it holds true $\0 \leq x^* \leq x(0)$.
		\qed
