\begin{abstract}
Projective monomial curves are associated with rings generated by monomials of equal degree in two variables. In this paper, we give an infinite class of non-smooth, non Cohen-Macaulay $k$-Buchsbaum projective monomial curves for any $k\geq 1$ and find the monomial generators for the respective  Macaulayfication. More generally, we demonstrate a method to find the Macaulayfication of a $k$-Buchsbaum monomial curve for any $k\geq 1$. We also discuss Castelnuovo-Mumford regularity of certain curves in terms of $k$-Buchsbaumness.    
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Let $\mathcal{K}$ be a field, a curve $\mathcal{C}$ is  a one-dimensional closed subscheme  of $\mathbb{P}^n$ which is locally Cohen-Macaulay and equidimensional. Let
\[
M(\mathcal{C}) = \bigoplus_{m \in \mathbb{Z}} H^1 (\mathbb{P}^n_\mathcal{K}, I_{\mathcal{C}}(m))
\]
be the Hartshorne-Rao module of $\mathcal{C}$. Here $I_{\mathcal{C}}$ is the ideal sheaf of $\mathcal{C}$. We know that $M(\mathcal{C}) \cong H^1_\mathfrak{M}(R)$ \cite{8} where $R = \mathcal{K}[x_0, \dots, x_n]/I(\mathcal{C})$, $I(\mathcal{C})$ is the homogeneous ideal of $\mathcal{C}$, $\mathfrak{M} = (x_0, \dots, x_n)R$ and $H^1_\mathfrak{M}(R)$ is the first local cohomology module with respect to $\mathfrak{M}$. We say $\mathcal{C}$ is strictly $k$-Buchsbaum if $k$ is the least number such that $\mathfrak{M}^k H^1_\mathfrak{M}(R) = 0$ and  $k$-Buchsbaum if $\mathfrak{M}^k H^1_\mathfrak{M}(R) = 0$. We say that $\mathcal{C}$ is arithmetically Cohen-Macaulay iff $k = 0$.  

In this paper, we are focusing on (projective) monomial curves. These curves are defined by their generic zero $(s^d, s^{d-a_1}t^{a_1}, \dots, s^{d-a_n}t^{a_n},t^d)$,
where $a_1 < a_2 < \dots < a_n < d$ are positive integers and gcd$(a_1, a_2, \dots, a_n, d) = 1.$ On the other hand, let  \( \mathcal{K}[M] \) be the subring of \( \mathcal{K}[s,t] \) generated by the set $ M = \{s^d, s^{d-a_1}t^{a_1}, \dots, s^{d-a_n}t^{a_n}, t^d\}$. If \( R \) is the homogeneous coordinate ring of the monomial curve, then \( R \cong \mathcal{K}[M] \). This curve often serves as a basic object for several problems. We can say this curve is smooth if 
\( a_1 = 1 \), \( a_n = d - 1 \) and it has at most one singular point if either \( a_1 = 1 \) or \( a_n = d - 1 \). There are very few known non-Cohen Macaulay rings \( R \) whose corresponding monomial curves are non-smooth \cite{6,9,10,11}. Another problem is to find out the Castelnuovo-Mumford regularity \( \operatorname{reg}(R) \). This problem is of great interest because \( \operatorname{reg}(R) \) is a measure for the complexity of the structure of graded modules. Several important invariants of graded rings and modules can be estimated by means of the regularity. In view of the Eisenbud-Goto conjecture \cite{12}, which was proved for projective curves by Gruson, Lazarsfeld and Peskine \cite{13} we know that \( \operatorname{reg}(R) \leq d - n \). L'vovsky gave a new bound in terms of gap \cite{14}. For smooth curves, Hellus, Hoa and  Stückrad \cite{15} showed that \( \operatorname{reg}(R) \) is bounded even by a fraction of the largest integer gap. There are also some explicit regularity formulas for the class of smooth and non-smooth monomial curves \cite{6,15,16}. Also, \( r_Q(R) \leq \operatorname{reg}(R) \), where \( r_Q(R) \) is the reduction number of \( R \) with respect to \( Q = (s^d, t^d) \). It is of interest to find curves for which  \( r_Q(R) = \operatorname{reg}(R) \) \cite{15}. Unfortunately, this equality does not hold in general, even in smooth monomial curves \cite{15}. Whenever it happens, it gives a combinatorial proof of the Eisenbud-Goto conjecture for monomial curves . This problem was recently solved by Nitsche \cite{17}. 

We call a ring \( R \subseteq \widetilde{R} \) a finite Macaulayfication of \( R \) if \( \bar{R} \) is a Cohen-Macaulay ring and \( \widetilde{R}/R \) has finite length. For an arbitrary set \( M \) of monomials, \( R \) always has a unique finite Macaulayfication \( \widetilde{R} \). This Macaulayfication helps us to estimate $\operatorname{reg}(R)$ and other invariants of $R$.
\\This paper is organized into six sections. In Section \ref{AE}, we present a large class of curves that are non-smooth, non-Cohen–Macaulay and Buchsbaum. Section \ref{BE} describes the Macaulayfication of $R$ in terms of the primary decomposition of an element. For Buchsbaum projective monomial curves in a general setup, this has been discussed in \cite{1}. Building on this, we determine the Macaulayfication of a broad class of monomial curves and provide infinitely many examples of strictly $2$-Buchsbaum rings. In Section \ref{CE}, we focus on regularity and extend several results from \cite{6}. We establish a relation between strictly $k$-Buchsbaum rings and the regularity of smooth monomial curves. Moreover, we identify a substantial class of examples where $r_Q(R) = \operatorname{reg}(R)$. In Section \ref{DE}, we compute examples of $k$-Buchsbaum monomial curves and their regularity using our results and we conclude with a question.

\section{Preliminaries}\label{section 1}
In this section, we gather some basic facts about homogeneous ideals and  projective closures of monomial curves. First, we recall that for an ideal $I\subseteq \mathcal{K}[x_1, \dots, x_n],$ 
 the \textit{homogenization} of $I$ is defined as the ideal
\[
I^h:= (f^h : f \in I) \subseteq \mathcal{K}[x_0, x_1, \dots, x_n],
\]
where $f^h$ denotes the homogenization of the polynomial $f$ .
Next, for an affine variety $W \subseteq \mathcal{K}^n$, the \textit{projective closure} of $W$ is the projective variety
\[
\overline{W}:= V(I_a(W)^h) \subset \mathbb{P}^n(\mathcal{K}),
\]
where $I_a(W)^h \subseteq \mathcal{K}[x_0, \dots, x_n]$ is the homogenization of the ideal $I_a(W) \subset \mathcal{K}[x_1, \dots, x_n]$ of $W$. It is well known that for an ideal $I \subseteq \mathcal{K}[x_1, \dots, x_n]$ ,
$V(I^h) \subseteq \mathbb{P}^n(k)$
is the projective closure of the affine variety $V_a(I) \subseteq \mathcal{K}^n$, see \cite[Chapter 8]{2}.
