\begin{lemma}\label{lemma reg}
Suppose $R_2' \subseteq R_{2l}$ or $R_2' \subseteq R_l R_1'$. Then $R$ is strictly $k$-Buchsbaum if and only if $a(\widetilde{R}/R) := \max\{n \in \mathbb{Z} \mid (\widetilde{R}/R)_n \neq 0\} = k + l - 1.$
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
We have that $\widetilde{R}$ is a graded $R$-module with grading $\widetilde{R} = \bigoplus_{i \geq 0} \widetilde{R}_i$, where
\[
\widetilde{R}_i = 
\begin{cases}
R_i & \text{for all } i < l, \\
R_l \oplus R_1' & \text{for } i = l, \\
R_{l+1} \oplus R_1 R_1' & \text{for } i = l+1, \\
\vdots & \\
R_{2l} \oplus R_l R_1' \oplus R_2' & \text{for } i = 2l,\\
\vdots
\end{cases}
\]
As $R_2' \subseteq R_{2l}$ or $R_2' \subseteq R_l R_1'$, we get that for all $i \geq 0,~
\widetilde{R}_{l+i} = R_{l+i} \oplus R_i R_1'$.
Since $R$ is strictly $k$-Buchsbaum and $H^1_{\mathfrak{M}}(R) \cong \widetilde{R}/R$ by Theorem \ref{H}, we have that $\mathfrak{M}^k H^1_{\mathfrak{M}}(R) = 0 \quad \text{but} \quad \mathfrak{M}^{k-1} H^1_{\mathfrak{M}}(R) \neq 0.$ Equivalently $\mathfrak{M}^k \widetilde{R} \subseteq R \quad \text{but} \quad \mathfrak{M}^{k-1} \widetilde{R} \nsubseteq R$
which implies $\widetilde{R}_{k+l} \subseteq R_{k+l} \quad \text{but} \quad \widetilde{R}_{k+l-1} \nsubseteq R_{k+l-1}.$
Therefore, the largest integer $n$ such that $(\widetilde{R}/R)_n \neq 0$ is $k + l - 1$, i.e., $a(\widetilde{R}/R) = k + l - 1$.
\end{proof}

