%where $\mathcal{I}$ is as defined in Section~\ref{AE}. Since $(x_1)^3(x_{n+3})^k \notin \mathcal{I}$, we obtain $m_1 + m_2 + m_3 + \sum_{i=0}^{n} n_i = k + 3$. 
On the other hand, clearly $P \notin (t^{a_n})$. Now, if possible $P \in (s^{a_n})$, then on comparing the degrees of $s$, we obtain that $3(a_n - b_1) - 2a_n = 2n - (r+1)$
must be even. Otherwise, we cannot express $2n - (r+1) \leq 2n + 1$ as a sum of elements from the set $A = \{2r + 2n - 1,\, r + 2n - 1,\, 2n + 1,\, 2n,\, 2n - 2,\, 2n - 4,\, \ldots,\, 2,\, 0\}$. Now suppose there exists $z \in A$ such that $s^{3a_n - 3b_1} = s^{2a_n} \cdot s^z$ in $\mathcal{R}'$. After homogenization, this gives  $s^{3a_n - 3b_1}t^{3b_1} \in (s^{a_n})\mathcal{R}$  which implies that $\mathcal{R}$ is Cohen–Macaulay, contradicting our assumption.  So, $P \notin (s^{a_n})$.
 This gives  $P\notin  Q\mathfrak{m}^{k+1}$, however
   $P \in \mathfrak{m}^{k+2}$ which means $r_Q(\mathcal{R})\geq k+2$. It is well known that $r_Q(\Tilde{\mathcal{R}}) \leq r_Q(\mathcal{R}) \leq \operatorname{reg}(\mathcal{R})$. So, the equality $\operatorname{reg}(\mathcal{R}) = r_Q(\mathcal{R})=r_Q(\Tilde{\mathcal{R}})$ follows from Corollary~\ref{c}.
\end{proof}
\section{Examples}\label{DE}
We complement our results with examples computed by Macaulay2 and present a problem based on our observations. 
\begin{example} Let $\mathcal{R}= \mathcal{K}[s^{21},\, s^{11}t^{10},\, s^{3}t^{18},\, s^{2}t^{19},\, t^{21}]$ We susbtitute $r=10$ and $n=1$ in Corollary \ref{XZ} to see that  $\mathcal{R}$ is strictly $2-$Buchsbaum ring of dimension two. Further, the Macaulayfication of $\mathcal{R}$ is $\mathcal{\widetilde{R}}=\mathcal{K}[s^{21}, s^{11}t^{10}, s^3t^{18}, s^{2}t^{19}, t^{21}, s^{12}t^{30}]$ by Remark \ref{k} and $\operatorname{reg}(\mathcal{R})=r_Q(R)=4.$ 
\end{example}
\begin{example}\label{n} Let 
$\mathcal{R}=\mathcal{K}[s^{13},s^8t^5, s^5t^8,s^4t^9,s^2t^{11},t^{13}]$.
 We susbtitute $r=5$ and $n=2$ in Thereom \ref{XY} to see that  $\mathcal{R}$ is non-Cohen-Macaulay Buchsbaum ring of dimension two. Further, the Macaulayfication of $\mathcal{R}$ is $\mathcal{\widetilde{R}}=\mathcal{K}[s^{13},s^8t^5, s^5t^8,s^4t^9,s^2t^{11},t^{13},s^{11}t^{15}]$ by Remark \ref{k} and $\operatorname{reg}(\mathcal{R})=r_Q(R)=3.$ 
 %%Castelnuovo-Mumford regularity of this curve is $3$.
\end{example}
\begin{example}
    Let $\mathcal{R}=\mathcal{K}[s^{15},s^10t^5, s^7t^8,s^6t^9,s^4t^{11},s^2t^{13},t^{15}]$.
 We susbtitute $r=5$ and $n=3$ in Thereom \ref{XY} to see that  $\mathcal{R}$ is a Cohen-Macaulay ring of dimension two. Further, $\operatorname{reg}(\mathcal{R})=r_Q(R)=3.$ 
\end{example}
Suppose $\mathcal{R}=\mathcal{K}[\mathcal{M}]$ be as in Section \ref{AE} . It follows from Corollary \ref{c}, that  $\operatorname{reg}(\mathcal{R})\geq k + 2.$ Our computations lead us to the following question for the class of projective monomial curves as defined in Section \ref{AE} for which we do not have any counter example.    
\begin{question}
If $\mathcal{R}$ is a strictly $k$-Buchsbaum ring for $k\geq 1$, is it true that $\operatorname{reg}(\mathcal{R})=k+2$?
\end{question}
