\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

The rapid growth of the Internet of Things (IoT) and smart applications in recent years has led to a massive increase in data generated by distributed sensing devices. According to a forecast by the International Data Corporation (IDC)~\cite{Reinsel2019}, there will be 55.9 billion IoT devices generating approximately 79.4 zettabytes (ZB) of data by 2025. Continuously transmitting this volume of data to centralized data centers or cloud platforms for processing is often infeasible due to several limitations, including network bandwidth constraints, increased latency, and potential privacy and security concerns. These challenges are particularly significant in applications that require real-time decision-making or operate in bandwidth-limited environments. As a response, \textit{edge computing} has emerged as a viable solution, enabling data processing to occur closer to the source--on or near the IoT devices using resource constrained single board computers such as Jetson, Raspberry Pi, and similar edge platforms. By offloading computation to the edge, this paradigm reduces communication overhead, lowers response times, and enhances the scalability and autonomy of intelligent systems.

Object detection, a key task in computer vision-based analytics, has seen widespread adoption across various domains including agriculture, sports analytics, transportation, healthcare, and smart cities. Recent advancements in machine learning and artificial intelligence (AI) have significantly improved the performance of object detection models. In particular, deep learning-based models such as \textit{You Only Look Once (YOLO)}~\cite{Redmon2016}, \textit{Single shot multibox detector (SSD)}~\cite{Liu2016} and \textit{EfficientDet}~\cite{Tan2020} have become increasingly prominent due to their high detection accuracy and real time capabilities. These models are often implemented using convolutional neural network (CNN) architectures and come in multiple variants to accommodate different computational budgets. For instance, the YOLOv8~\cite{ultralytics2024} family includes \textit{nano}, \textit{small}, \textit{medium}, and \textit{large} versions each reflecting trade-offs between model size, speed, and accuracy.

%While many models have been optimized for deployment on resource-constrained edge devices, others are designed for high-performance computing environments. These optimizations inevitably affect the performance of the models, particularly in terms of detection accuracy. In the object detection domain, the \textit{mean Average Precision (mAP)} is a widely accepted metric used to evaluate and compare model accuracy. Selecting a suitable model–device configuration thus involves careful consideration of trade-offs between accuracy, latency, and energy consumption.

