The dynamic nature of object detection in real-world environments presents both a challenge and an opportunity. Visual scenes vary widely in complexity—from sparsely populated streets at night to dense daytime urban intersections—leading to fluctuating computational demands for object detection. In such contexts, the heterogeneity of edge resources, including both hardware devices and detection models, offers an opportunity for intelligent allocation of requests to device–model pairs. 

Our recent findings show that detection models exhibit notably different energy, latency, and accuracy profiles when deployed across various edge devices~\cite{alqahtani2024}. By leveraging this hardware–model heterogeneity, systems can achieve substantial energy savings without compromising detection accuracy or latency.

Energy efficiency is particularly critical on edge devices operating under strict power constraints, such as those relying on batteries or local energy harvesting methods like solar panels. Yet in safety-critical applications like surveillance and traffic monitoring, accuracy remains non-negotiable. This creates the central challenge addressed in this study: ``\textit{how can we minimize energy usage without degrading detection quality?}''

The solution lies in adaptive, context-aware routing strategies that dynamically select the optimal device–model pair based on scene complexity. Simpler scenes allow for lightweight processing, while complex environments require more accurate--and consequently more energy-intensive--models. To address this, we propose the \toolname framework, which introduces a set of dynamic, novel, intelligent routing algorithms to redirect an image processing task to the most appropriate device-model pair. The goal is to reduce energy consumption while maintaining high detection accuracy. 

ECORE's routers operate by applying lightweight complexity estimation techniques during the pre-processing stage to infer the number of objects in each incoming image. Based on this estimation, the router selects and forwards the request to an optimal model–device pair.

The main contributions of this work are as follows:
\begin{enumerate}
\item We propose a complete system architecture for a framework called \toolname, optimized routing of image processing tasks to a group of heterogeneous edge devices running diverse object detection models, coordinated by a central gateway responsible for intelligent routing decisions.
\item We develop three object count estimation approaches to support intelligent routing decisions in \toolname. The first is an edge detection–based approach, which utilizes the Canny technique to estimate the number of objects in an image. The second is an SSD-based front-end approach, which deploys a lightweight object detection model at the gateway to make decisions. The third is an outputs-based approach, which leverages the object detection results returned from the backbend in the previous processing round and uses this information to inform routing decisions for the subsequent request.
%\item We develop a dynamic routing algorithm based on a greedy heuristic that selects the optimal model–device pair for each image by leveraging profiling data collected across different object count groups and device–model combinations. The algorithm aims to achieve a balance between energy efficiency and detection accuracy.
\item We conduct a comprehensive experimental evaluation of our proposed routing approaches by comparing them against both an ideal benchmark--an Oracle Router that knows the exact number of objects in the image--and several baseline techniques, including Random, Round Robin, Lowest Energy Consumption, Lowest Inference Time, and Highest mean Average Precision (mAP). The evaluation is conducted on a real-world testbed using multiple datasets, including the Common Objects in Context (COCO) validation set and a short video, under diverse input scenarios.

\end{enumerate}

