\section{Motivation}
\label{sec:motivation}
 This section highlights the motivation for developing adaptive, context-aware routing strategies in edge-based object detection systems. Figure~\ref{fig:scene_examples} illustrates two contrasting scenes captured by an urban surveillance camera at an intersection. The first image depicts numerous pedestrians crossing the intersection, while the second image shows the same location when it is sparsely populated. In the former high density situation, accurate object detection becomes more critical and potentially more computationally demanding, especially in safety sensitive applications such as surveillance, traffic monitoring, and crowd management. Failure to detect or correctly classify objects in this context can compromise system reliability and lead to safety risks. In contrast, the latter image shows a sparsely populated street with minimal environmental activity and few objects of interests. In such low activity scenarios, the computational complexity of achieving reasonable accuracy is reduced, and the risk of critical misdetections is substantially lower. Therefore, energy savings can be achieved by using lighter detection models, particularly when edge devices operate under battery or solar power constraints.

These scenarios underscore the importance of a dynamic routing approach that forwards requests to the optimal edge node based on scene complexity. By estimating the complexity of a scene prior to routing, the system can intelligently choose between high accuracy models for crowded scenes and energy efficient models for sparse scenes. This motivation drives the design of our proposed routing algorithm, which aims to optimize energy consumption without compromising accuracy where it matters most.

