\section{System Design and Implementation}
\label{sec:system design}
This section describes the system architecture of \toolname, designed for energy-efficient and performance-aware routing of object detection tasks in an edge–IoT environment. Figure~\ref{system_arch} illustrates the overall design. We assume a scenario where a pool of backend edge nodes hosts a variety of model–device pairs. Each backend device runs a dedicated object detection model and exposes an inference API to process images or video frames captured via distributed cameras or embedded sensors. This modular and distributed architecture ensures scalability and responsiveness, making it suitable for real-time applications in energy-constrained and performance-critical edge computing scenarios.

Cameras or embedded sensors transmit image data or video frames to a central gateway, which performs lightweight preprocessing and analysis. As shown in Figure~\ref{system_arch}, the gateway first applies one of the proposed object estimation techniques to infer the number of objects in each incoming image. This count is passed to the routing algorithm (Algorithm~\ref{alg:routing}), which uses it to determine the most appropriate model–device pair.

The routing algorithm begins by identifying the corresponding object count group based on the estimated number of objects, using a predefined set of group rules defined by numeric ranges and labels. It then filters the profiling data to retain only the entries that match the identified group. From this subset, it calculates the maximum achievable mAP and derives a minimum acceptable mAP by subtracting a predefined margin. The data is then filtered again to retain only model–device pairs whose mAP falls within this target range. Among these, the pair with the lowest energy consumption is selected. The gateway forwards the request to the selected endpoint, which processes the image and returns the detected objects and their bounding boxes.

