\subsubsection{Balanced Sorted Dataset Results}
This section highlights the results of evaluating our proposed routing approaches and baseline routing strategies using the balanced sorted dataset as shown in Figure~\ref{fig:results_sorted_balanced_dataset}. With respect to dynamic energy consumption Figure~\ref{fig:results_sorted_balanced_dataset}(b), \textbf{LE} demonstrates the most energy-efficient performance, consuming only 227~mWh and establishing the theoretical lower bound. \textbf{LI} performs similarly, with only a slight increase in consumption. At the opposite end, \textbf{HMG} exhibits a substantial rise in energy usage—approximately 50\% higher than the baseline—while \textbf{SF} records more than double the energy consumption of \textbf{LE}. The \textbf{Orc} router shows a 40\% increase in energy use relative to the lower bound. Among the proposed approaches, \textbf{ED} and \textbf{OB} consume 64\% and 45\% more energy, respectively. In comparison, the baseline methods \textbf{RR} and \textbf{Rnd} result in increases of roughly 70\% and 62\%.

Latency results, presented in Figure~\ref{fig:results_sorted_balanced_dataset}(c), show that \textbf{LI} achieves the lowest total latency at approximately 306 seconds, with \textbf{LE} following closely behind, incurring only a 2-second increase. \textbf{HM} and \textbf{SF} exhibit the highest latency overheads, with increases of around 77\% and 81\%, respectively. \textbf{HMG} also shows a notable rise, adding approximately 34\% to the baseline latency. In contrast, \textbf{Orc} and \textbf{OB} introduce modest increases of about 9\%, while \textbf{ED} shows a slightly higher increase of 13\%. The \textbf{RR} and \textbf{Rnd} routers fall in the mid-range, with latency increases of 18\% and 22\%, respectively.

In terms of detection accuracy (mAP), Figure~\ref{fig:results_sorted_balanced_dataset}(a) indicates that \textbf{HMG} achieves the highest score at 40.94, establishing the upper performance bound. \textbf{Orc}, \textbf{SF}, and \textbf{OB} closely follow, each exhibiting a negligible decline of less than 1\%. \textbf{ED} also shows minor reductions of approximately 1\%. In contrast, \textbf{RR} and \textbf{Rnd} suffer notable accuracy losses of around 18\%, while \textbf{LE} and \textbf{LI} demonstrate the most substantial declines, with reductions of approximately 30\% and 40\%, respectively.

\begin{insight}
  Among the proposed routing methods, \textbf{OB} offers the best overall trade-off, closely approaching oracle-level performance in both accuracy and latency while achieving the lowest energy consumption of all proposed approaches. This makes \textbf{OB} the most suitable choice for sequential image processing scenarios.
\end{insight}

