\subsection{Discussions}

\toolname demonstrates promising results by reducing energy consumption and latency while maintaining strong accuracy. While the \textbf{Orc} router represents an idealized baseline, benefiting from prior knowledge of object counts, achieving comparably strong results in practice is not infeasible. Our proposed routing approaches—\textbf{ED} and \textbf{OB}—achieve performance close to Oracle, with marginal differences. However, the effectiveness of each method is dataset-dependent: \textbf{OB} performs optimally in sequential and structured scenarios, such as the sorted balanced and video datasets, while \textbf{ED} is better suited for static image inputs without any continuity. 

The current design assumes static profiling, where performance metrics remain stable at runtime, which may not reflect real-world dynamics such as temperature, background load, or battery state. Additionally, our heuristic operates at single-request granularity, limiting its applicability in batch or load-balancing contexts. It is important to note that the proposed approaches do not support multi-objective optimization; balancing trade-offs between energy and latency would require a Pareto-optimal or weighted strategy, where a greedy algorithm may no longer suffice.


\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related work}
This section discusses related studies and compares them with our proposed work. Ji et al.~\cite{Ji2025} introduce a Cloud-Edge Large-Tiny Model Collaborative (CELTC) architecture that employs deep reinforcement learning to enhance surveillance data processing and decision-making in offshore drilling platform environments. The CELTC framework integrates edge–cloud computing by deploying high-accuracy large models on cloud servers and lightweight, efficient models on edge devices to balance computational demand and responsiveness. Similarly, Trinh et al.~\cite{Trinh2018} explore the use of Mobile Edge Computing (MEC) to address energy management challenges in power-constrained IoT devices while enabling low-latency processing of high-resolution visual data. Focusing on facial recognition for disaster response scenarios, the authors propose an offload decision-making algorithm that dynamically determines whether to process tasks at the edge or offload them to the cloud, based on current workload and energy constraints. While these studies present valuable edge–cloud collaborative architectures, their focus differs from ours. Specifically, they rely on cloud resources to support edge processing, whereas our work emphasizes fully distributed collaboration among multiple edge devices. Furthermore, our approach directly targets the optimization of energy consumption and latency in edge environments without compromising detection accuracy, making it well-suited for real-time applications where both efficiency and performance are critical. 

Tu et al.~\cite{Tu2023} propose EdgeLeague, a framework designed to manage multiple video streams with diverse Quality of Service (QoS) requirements, ensuring reliable surveillance performance under constrained edge resources and fluctuating uplink bandwidth. EdgeLeague leverages collaborative edge computing and optimizes camera network configurations to solve an NP-hard integer nonlinear problem by dynamically adjusting camera resolutions and selecting appropriate detection models across cooperating edge nodes. Similarly, Zhang et al.~\cite{Zhang2022} address configuration selection and bandwidth allocation for multiple video streams connected to a shared uplink channel through a common edge node. The authors propose JCAB, an efficient online algorithm that jointly optimizes configuration adaptation and bandwidth distribution. The approach is further extended to a multi-edge scenario, allowing individual users or video streams to select their preferred edge node, thereby enhancing flexibility and resource utilization. While these studies focus on optimizing system performance by adapting hardware configurations and bandwidth allocation, our approach takes a different direction. Instead of adjusting device settings, we analyze the content of incoming images and dynamically route requests to the most suitable edge device–model pair. This enables energy-efficient inference while preserving detection accuracy, offering a lightweight and scalable alternative to hardware-level reconfiguration strategies.

Tundo et al.~\cite{Tundo2023} introduces an energy-conscious strategy for designing and deploying self-adaptive, AI-driven applications, aiming to balance application-level objectives—such as object detection accuracy and frame processing rate—with energy consumption. The authors address the challenge of identifying optimal system configurations for self-adaptation using a meta-heuristic search method that requires minimal empirical sampling. Similarly, Matathammal et al.~\cite{matathammal2025edgemlbalancer} present EdgeMLBalancer, a self-adaptive methodology designed to optimize CPU utilization and resource management on edge devices. Their approach dynamically switches between deployed models based on real-time CPU usage monitoring across processor cores. In Kulkarni et al.~\cite{Kulkarni2023}, the concept of a Machine Learning Model Balancer is proposed to manage uncertainty in ML model outputs through the coordinated use of multiple models. Building on this idea, the authors introduce AdaMLS, which enhances the traditional MAPE-K (Monitor-Analyze-Plan-Execute over a Knowledge base) loop to enable continuous adaptation of machine learning systems. The study in Marda et al.~\cite{Marda2024} introduces SWITCH, a framework designed to improve system-level adaptability by enabling runtime model switching. SWITCH functions as a general-purpose web service applicable to various ML scenarios, and is specifically demonstrated in an object detection use case. Whereas these studies focus on self-adaptive techniques that switch between models within a single edge device, our work adopts a broader architectural perspective. We design a system that maintains a pool of edge devices and model variants, and introduces a dynamic routing mechanism to select the most appropriate device–model pair for each image. This approach supports distributed, energy-efficient processing across multiple edge nodes while preserving detection accuracy, extending adaptability beyond local device-level switching to collaborative edge inference. 

Recent research has explored various routing strategies to improve the efficiency and adaptability of Large Language Model (LLM) inference. Stripelis et al.~\cite{stripelis2024tensoropera} introduce a modular querying system that dynamically dispatches incoming queries to specialized LLM experts based on the query’s requirements. Similarly, Maurya et al.~\cite{maurya2024selectllm} propose an algorithm that selects the most appropriate subset of LLMs from a larger model pool to optimize performance and cost. Zheng et al.~\cite{zheng2025citer} present CITER, a fine-grained token-level routing framework that enables collaborative inference between small and large language models by directing low-importance tokens to smaller models for efficiency and reserving complex tokens for larger models to maintain accuracy. Guha et al.~\cite{NEURIPS2024_e6b57a99} introduce Smoothie, a routing method based on weak supervision that constructs a latent variable graphical model from the embeddings of multiple LLM responses, enabling accurate output estimation without labeled data. Mohammadshahi et al.~\cite{mohammadshahi2024routoo} offers Routoo, a flexible routing architecture that balances latency, cost, and inference quality by allowing users to control trade-offs according to task requirements. Sikeridis et al.~\cite{sikeridis2024pickllm} propose PickLLM which employs a reinforcement learning–based framework that dynamically assigns queries to models using a reward function guided by query-specific metrics such as accuracy, latency, and cost. While these works demonstrate significant advances in LLM routing, they are primarily focused on natural language processing tasks in cloud-based environments. In contrast, our work addresses object detection in edge computing settings, with a particular emphasis on minimizing energy consumption and processing latency and accuracy factors that are critical for real-time, resource-constrained applications.


\section{Conclusions and Future Direction}
\label{sec:conclusions}


Deploying object detection models on resource-constrained edge devices—particularly those powered by batteries or sustainable energy sources such as solar panels—requires careful consideration of energy consumption. In many real-world applications, especially safety-critical systems, the operating environment is highly dynamic, and the number of objects captured in each image can vary significantly. This variability can substantially affect the accuracy of object detection models, necessitating adaptive strategies that balance efficiency and performance.

To address these challenges, we proposed \toolname along with three dynamic, estimation-based routing algorithms that first estimate the number of objects in an image using lightweight pre-processing techniques. Then, based on this estimation, the algorithms route the request to the most suitable edge model-device pair. The routing decision is informed by profiling and is designed to minimize overall energy consumption while preserving detection accuracy within acceptable range.

Our experimental evaluation demonstrates that the proposed routing strategy performs favorably compared to baseline approaches. Specifically, it achieves a substantial reduction in energy usage while maintaining accuracy levels within an acceptable margin. The results highlight the potential of dynamic, context-aware routing for improving the energy-efficiency of AI workloads at the edge.

\textbf{Future Work:} To enhance the robustness of \toolname, future work will explore dynamic profiling to account for runtime variability such as temperature, battery state, and background load. We also aim to extend the routing strategy to support batch-level decision-making for better load balancing. Additionally, incorporating multi-objective optimization techniques, such as Pareto-based or weighted approaches, will allow more flexible trade-offs between energy consumption and latency across diverse deployment scenarios.


