\section*{Appendix: The Principle of Redundant Reflection for Improper Priors}

\;

Suppose that the distribution of the future outcomes is an i.i.d normal distribution with known variance $\sigma^2=1$ and unknown mean $\theta$. Then Jeffreys' prior (which is improper) is given by\begin{align*}
    p(\theta)= 1,
\end{align*} and the posterior density obtained by Bayes' Theorem for the contemplated dataset $y^\star=(y^\star_1,\dots,y^\star_n)$ of size $n$ is given by\begin{align*}
    p(\theta|y^\star)=\mathcal{N}\left(\theta;\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^ny^\star_i,\frac{1}{n}\right)=\mathcal{N}\left(\theta;\Bar{y}^\star,\frac{1}{n}\right),
\end{align*} the normal distribution with mean equal to the mean of the data and variance equal to $\frac{1}{n}$. Just like the posterior, the marginal density can be derived from Bayes' Theorem and is equal to the improper density \begin{align*}
    p(y^\star)=\int p(\theta)p(y^\star|\theta)\;d\theta=1.
\end{align*} However, taking expectations with respect to this improper density is still possible, and we do indeed have that \begin{align*}
    \mathbb{E}_{y^\star\sim p(y^\star)}[p(\theta|y^\star)]=\int p(\theta|y^\star)p(y^\star)\;dy^\star=1=p(\theta).
\end{align*}

Since the credibility intervals obtained from Jeffreys' prior coincide with frequentist confidence intervals in this case, this is a good opportunity to observe whether frequentist testing follows the Principle of Redundant Reflection, that is, whether one would expect a frequentist test to reject the null hypothesis given this objective prior. Indeed, the expected likelihood-ratio test statistic of a both-sided Gauss test against $H_0:\theta=0$ has expectation \begin{align*}
    \mathbb{E}_{y^\star\sim p(y^\star)}\left[-2\log\frac{p(y^\star|\theta=0)}{p(y^\star|\theta=\hat{\theta}_{\text{MLE}})}\right]=\int-2\log\frac{\prod^n_{i=1}\mathcal{N}(y^\star_i;0,1)}{\prod^n_{i=1}\mathcal{N}(y^\star_i;\Bar{y}^\star,1)}\;dy^\star=\infty,
\end{align*} so one would expect an increasing number of positive test results when repeating this test. On the other hand the expected Bayes Factor \begin{align*}
    \mathbb{E}_{y^\star\sim p(y^\star)}\left[\frac{p(y^\star|\theta=0)}{p(y^\star)}\right]=\int \frac{\mathcal{N}(y^\star;0,1)}{p(y^\star)}p(y^\star)\;dy^\star=1,
\end{align*}so a Bayesian neither expects evidence for, nor against the null given the alternative hypothesis. 

Of course, an exact probabilistic interpretation of this result is made difficult by the fact that the distribution involved is improper. However, the Principle of Redundant Reflection also holds after the improper prior has been updated to a proper one, as was pointed out in this article. The interpretation is more straightforward in that setting. It should also be mentioned that this setting is somewhat artificial, since the variance of the data is usually unknown in addition to the mean. For an exploration of the relationship between frequentist and Bayesian testing in a more realistic setting we refer the interested reader to \citet{Wagenmakers2022p}.

