\section{Minimum cost flow equivalence}
\label{app:minimu_cost_equivalence}

Following the notation in \cite{Ahuja_book}, a potential on a network is an assignment of real values on its nodes. We say that a potential $\phi$ is a topological ordering if following any edges in the network increases the potential. That is, $\phi (i)<\phi(j)$ for each edge $ij\in \mathcal E_{\mathbf w}$. Not all networks have topological orderings; in fact, it is not hard to show that that acyclic networks are precisely the ones that have a topological ordering \cite[p.77]{Ahuja_book}.
%OLD:  Due to this ordering of the nodes some authors refer to these networks as purely hierarchical ones \cite{Gupte_2011, Luo_2011}.

Potentials help us find optimal solutions to \eqref{eq:x_constrains} thanks to the following result \cite[Theorem 9.4]{Ahuja_book}. 
\begin{lem}
A network $\mathbf x ^*$ satisfying \eqref{eq:x_constrains} is a minimum of \eqref{eq:min_x} iff there exists a potential $\phi$ in $\mathbf w$ such that
\begin{subequations}
